Below is a quick‑start guide on **how to pull out every `<Element>` child** from your `SAMLAssertionNode`.  
I’ll give you two options:

1. A *plain‑Java* solution that works with the standard W3C DOM API (`org.w3c.dom`).  
2. How to invoke the helper function you mentioned – the `getChildren()` wrapper – and interpret its output.

---

### 1️⃣ Plain‑Java DOM approach  

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import org.w3c.dom.*;

public List<Element> extractAllElementsFrom(SAMLAssertionNode samlRoot) {
    List<Element> elementChildren = new ArrayList<>();

    // Walk through each direct child of the root node
    for (Node child = samlRoot.getFirstChild();
         child != null; 
         child = child.getNextSibling()) {

        if (child instanceof Element) {          // <-- only Elements
            elementChildren.add((Element) child); // cast safely
        }
    }

    return elementChildren;
}
```

#### What this does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| `samlRoot.getFirstChild()` | Gets the first sibling under the assertion node. |
| Loop over siblings (`nextSibling`) | Traverses all immediate children. |
| `instanceof Element` | Filters out text nodes, comments, CDATA sections, etc., keeping only real XML elements. |

If you’re dealing with nested structures and want *all* descendant `<Element>` instances (not just direct children), replace the loop body with an XPath query like:

```java
XPath xpath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();
String expr   = ".//*/";           // All descendants that are Elements
NodeList nodes = (NodeList)xpath.compile(expr)
                                 .evaluate(samlRoot, XPathConstants.NODESET);

List<Element> allDescendants = IntStream.range(0, nodes.getLength())
                                       .mapToObj(nodes::item)
                                       .filter(Element.class::isInstance)
                                       .map(node -> (Element) node)
                                       .collect(Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new));
```

---

